WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.735 --> 00:00:06.030
For the last like 50 or 60 years,

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:10.070
space has mostly been dominated
by a handful of governments.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.500
It took government money
to get a rocket up,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.713
to build a satellite.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:20.400
Around sort of 2008, the
launch of this Falcon 1 rocket,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.690
made by SpaceX, that marked the first time

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:27.120
we had billionaires who
had some success in space.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.670
We have lift off.

00:00:31.143 --> 00:00:32.430
And now we're at this
really interesting moment

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.730
in time where I think pretty
much inspired by this example,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.440
everybody decided they
could do space as well

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.560
and could take a crack at these things

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.130
that people have been dreaming
about for almost a century.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.600
We've gone from
governments to billionaires

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.570
to now all this private
money, venture capitalists,

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.640
just regular old millionaires
flooding in to space

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.250
and funding all kinds of projects.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:58.910
As space becomes more accessible

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.500
to private enterprise,
many companies are hoping

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.320
to make it not just
possible but affordable

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.620
to put objects into orbit.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.680
Now, a rocket startup called Astra is

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.390
on the verge of launching rockets

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.410
at a fraction of the
cost of its competitors,

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.110
potentially opening up a new era

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.000
in the technology and business of space.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.940
As I look 50 years into the future,

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.610
I see a platform emerging in space

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.830
and right above that thin
blue line, I see competition,

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.950
I see ecosystems that are
global, I see standards emerging

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.390
and I see a really exciting opportunity

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.623
for a trillion plus
dollar economy to form.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.170
My name's Chris Kemp, I'm the founder,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:48.160
chairman and CEO of Astra and
we're in our rocket factory,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.210
located just across the Bay Bridge

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.310
from San Francisco in Alameda, California.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.299
And I think that Astra is really

00:01:56.299 --> 00:01:57.350
offering a very unique service.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.400
No one can go anywhere on earth

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.730
and put something anywhere in space

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.330
in a few days with a few people.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.040
We started talking
about basically the idea

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.250
of building a rocket factory
from the very beginning

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.110
and this idea was based on the principle

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.400
that if you make a lot of something,

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.310
the cost comes down a lot

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.350
so we're building Hondas not Ferraris.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.630
We don't use any of the exotic materials,

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.560
carbon fiber composites, 3D printing,

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.820
that a lot of other companies
like to talk a lot about.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:28.970
They're actually very expensive

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.790
and they're very hard to scale

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.524
so we're building these
rockets out of aluminum

00:02:33.524 --> 00:02:35.510
so we're bending, we're
welding, we're fastening,

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.920
we're stamping, we're machining aluminum

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.460
but our thesis was that
if you could build rockets

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.690
like an aircraft or an automobile,

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.460
we could really bring the economics

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.560
in line with some of these larger rockets.

00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:48.817
Astra's entering

00:02:48.817 --> 00:02:51.330
into a crowded and well-funded space.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.600
SpaceX is the clear
leader with its Falcon 9,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.603
Falcon Heavy and Falcon
Super Heavy rockets.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.480
Another front runner is New
Zealand based Rocket Lab

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.593
with their Electron and Neutron rockets.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.654
Astra has announced two different models,

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:10.430
one small, one big and says
both will be much faster

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:13.560
and cheaper to build than
those of its competitors.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.120
For like 100 years, people
in the United States,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.730
Germany, Russia have
been trying to build one

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.820
of these small liquid fueled rockets.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.480
The first private company

00:03:25.319 --> 00:03:27.890
to really pull it off was SpaceX in 2008.

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:31.820
It took SpaceX seven
years to go from nothing

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:33.540
to getting this first rocket up.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.270
Astra's whole goal really was to try

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.290
and shrink that time as much as possible

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.810
and to develop and manufacture this type

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.540
of rocket faster than
any company in history.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.020
Over the next few years
here at this facility,

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.450
we'll be increasing the
production rate of rockets

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:55.000
from monthly this year to
weekly in 2023 to bi-weekly

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
in 2024 to a full daily
production rate in 2025

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.290
and by launching a rocket a day,

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:04.820
we'll be able to bring
the price point down

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.640
to about a half a million dollars

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.180
and we'll be selling launches

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.393
for a few million dollars that can deliver

00:04:10.393 --> 00:04:12.153
between three and 500
kilograms to low-earth orbit.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.640
Astra's ambition was born

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.190
from the massive demand for
getting small satellites

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.540
into low earth orbit.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.900
Startups are lining up to
hitch rides on bigger rockets,

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.253
often waiting months or
years for their turn.

00:04:26.482 --> 00:04:27.315
Well, about five years ago,

00:04:27.315 --> 00:04:29.210
one of my best friends started Planet,

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:32.340
which was the pioneer in
building small satellites

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.270
and they were having some
real trouble getting to space.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:36.370
After spending a few months,

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:38.150
meeting a bunch of the
other rocket companies,

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.566
it was pretty clear that
he needed to be able

00:04:40.566 --> 00:04:42.320
to get from anywhere on
earth to anywhere in space

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.670
on his schedule, not the
schedule of some large satellite.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.340
If you're a small satellite company

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.750
and you want to get on a SpaceX rocket,

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.610
the going rate for a SpaceX launch

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.680
is about $60 million
and there's gonna be one

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.820
or two very large satellites
that are on that rocket

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.270
and they're really the primary payload.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.340
All these startups want to be these,

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:04.770
what they call secondary payloads.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.500
They're sort of attached
around the big satellites

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.240
and so you're paying almost to be

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.870
like a second class
citizen on this rocket.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.190
The large satellites, the
people that are really paying

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:18.450
for the bulk of the launch,
they get deposited exactly

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.800
in the orbit that they want.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:23.760
Then the secondary
satellites get dropped off

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:27.890
and usually they have to
use really small thrusters

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:30.700
on the satellites then to
kind of push themselves

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.660
into their desired orbit.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:34.090
There's not as many guarantees

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.140
that you're going to end
up in the right spot.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.120
Companies like Planet Labs are aiming

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.160
to dispatch many tiny
satellites all around the globe

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.600
in what's called a constellation.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.190
The satellites can do
various computational tasks

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.230
using data from all over the earth

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.020
and since they're tiny and
sit in low earth orbit,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.500
they just burn up in the atmosphere

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:55.570
after a while, rather than contributing

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.743
to the growing problem of space debris.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.640
As more and more companies try
to build systems like this,

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.580
the need for easy access to space

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.430
is becoming greater and greater.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.660
That's where Astra comes in.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:09.530
The idea with the small rockets is

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:11.930
that you're getting
your own ride to space.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:15.950
It's taking your satellite
to exactly the orbit you want

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:18.680
and even more than that, you're
not having to wait months

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.030
and months and months to
find space on a launch.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.380
The whole idea here with
something like Astra

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:27.340
is you hop on a website,
you book a rocket launch,

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.650
you send them your satellite and hopefully

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:31.960
in a couple of weeks, your
satellite's up in orbit

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:33.210
so we've never seen anything

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.627
like this sort of access to space

00:06:35.627 --> 00:06:38.880
and the pace at which you
could fly something into space

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.390
In much the same way,
Amazon has big warehouses

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.540
and trucks that pull up.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.690
You wouldn't land an Airbus 380

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.230
on Market Street and
deliver a package to me.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.030
A little sprinter van pulls up

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:53.110
and someone walks up
and delivers a package.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.340
Just look back at the last 10 years.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:58.680
We've seen almost 400
companies now formed,

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.130
solving all sorts of really
interesting problems,

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:03.780
looking at the earth, looking
at how crops are growing,

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:05.790
looking at where we're fishing oceans,

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:09.050
looking at how to prevent
things like coral reefs

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.450
being overheated in the oceans

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.480
and understanding weather patterns.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.330
I can't imagine all the applications.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.691
While private space flight

00:07:16.691 --> 00:07:19.090
is becoming an increasingly crowded field.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:22.130
The success rate is still extremely low.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:25.070
More than 20 rocket
startups have now failed

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.730
and there are at least 80 others

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.120
currently trying to get off the ground.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.870
Only two private companies,
SpaceX and Rocket Lab,

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:35.350
are flying regular missions to space

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:38.330
and Astra could be on the
verge of becoming the third.

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:42.800
So Astra's been at it for
about four or five years.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.070
They've done really well in many senses.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:48.100
They actually got to Alaska
and did their first launch

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.420
in about two years, which was a record.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:54.403
Rocket launch in Kodiak,
Alaska, pretty cool.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:01.593
The downside of that launch
was that the rocket blew up.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:05.430
Whoa.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:07.410
It took 'em about a couple more tries

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.930
and then just at the end of last year,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:13.480
they had really what was,
for all intents and purposes,

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.160
a tremendous success,
their rocket got like just

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.880
to the edge of low earth
orbit within really a few feet

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.263
and so we know now that
their rocket works.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:26.493
The company plans

00:08:26.493 --> 00:08:28.630
to launch another rocket later this year

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:31.950
as they continue to inch towards
making commercial flights.

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:35.150
Eventually, they hope to launch
a rocket every single day

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.710
which could lead to an
exponential increase

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.560
in the number of satellites in orbit.

00:08:40.535 --> 00:08:42.129
I mean, we're at this
super interesting time

00:08:42.129 --> 00:08:44.380
that I don't think most people
are paying attention to,

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:47.920
which is that at the start of this year,

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.470
there are probably about
2200 satellites roughly

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:52.870
orbiting the earth.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:55.240
If you look at the launch manifests

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:58.190
of the various rocket
and satellite companies,

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:01.729
that number is supposed
to go to about 50,000

00:09:01.729 --> 00:09:06.060
to a 100,000 satellites in
the next five to 10 years.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.880
This is the next great technology story,

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.070
the next great computing
infrastructure story

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:14.850
is what's going to happen
right above our heads

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.910
and so the next few
years are going to bring

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:21.910
with them this unbelievable
frenetic amount of activity,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:25.950
both from the rocket companies
and the satellite companies,

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:28.760
and really all the global powers kind of

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:31.310
understanding the new rules of engagement

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.180
that go in to this era when governments

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.792
do not control this real
estate that had been

00:09:37.792 --> 00:09:40.320
the most prized, expensive
real estate there is.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.580
Now, just about anyone with
a decent amount of money

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:48.340
and a decent amount of
brains can get to space

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:50.020
and do something interesting there.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:51.010
Our customers will be able to go

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:53.230
from a concept to a constellation

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.030
to really helping improve life on earth

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:57.360
in months instead of many years

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:58.710
and that's really our purpose

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:00.480
and I think over the next few years,

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.510
you'll see us really make
it easier to allow space

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:07.313
to be a platform for
entrepreneurs across the world.

